### Radio Observations

We checked radio sources located within R_{95} using the second data release of the Rapid ASKAP Continuum Survey (RACS-mid; Duchesne et al., 2024). At the central wavelength of 1.36 GHz, this Survey covers the whole sky south of +49{}^{\\circ} declination and has a median angular resolution of 11{}^{\\prime\\prime}.2\\times9{}^{\\prime\\prime}.3 though it varies to maximize sky coverage and sensitivity across the covered area. The cutout image covering the region of interest was downloaded from the RACS data server2 and overplotted on the Swift-XRT and the VR-band optical image taken with the Dark Energy Camera (DECAM; Abbott et al., 2021). A complex, diffuse radio morphology was seen overlapping with _"Kathryn's Wheel"_ though the central galaxy, ESO 179-13, remain undetected down to 0.45 mJy/beam, i.e., 3\\sigma detection limit (Figure 1, bottom panel). None of the identified X-ray sources exhibit a radio morphology typically observed in an AGN, thus ruling out the possibility of them being jetted AGN (e.g., Ackermann et al., 2011). A bright radio object (F_{\\nu,\\rm 1.36GHz}\\sim 67 mJy) was found at 4.7{}^{\\prime} eastwards from LEDA 3080069 (Figure 1, top right panel). This object is unlikely to be the blazar counterpart of 4FGL J1647.5-5724 because: (i) it is outside of the updated R_{95}, (ii) it remained undetected in Swift-XRT observations, (iii) its optical counterpart (RA=252{}^{\\circ}.004979, Dec=-57{}^{\\circ}.405146) shows a large proper motion of 9.1 mas year{}^{-1} in Gaia observations (Gaia Collaboration et al., 2021). indicating a possible Galactic origin, and (iv) in the WISE color-color diagram, it occupies a region far from that is populated by \\gamma-ray emitting blazars (Massaro et al., 2012).

A detailed morphological study of _"Kathryn's Wheel"_ was carried out by Parker et al. (2015). The source was observed as a part of the SuperCOSMOS H\\alpha Survey of the Southern Galactic plane (Parker et al., 2005) which revealed the central galaxy, ESO 179-13, to be surrounded by a ring of star-forming knots and having 6.1 kpc diameter. Based on the H\\alpha and mid-infrared flux measurements, the integrated star-formation rate (SFR) of the whole system is reported to be 0.2-0.5 M{}_{\\odot} year{}^{-1}, higher than that of Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC; Parker et al., 2015). The most luminous Hii region in the system has the H\\alpha luminosity, \\simeq 5\\times 10^{39} erg s{}^{-1}, comparable to supergiant Hii region 30 Doradus in LMC, which is also a bright \\gamma-ray emitter (Abdo et al., 2010). These results provide supportive evidence that the vigorous star-forming activities of _"Kathryn's Wheel"_ could be responsible for the observed \\gamma-ray emission, thereby making it a plausible counterpart of the \\gamma-ray source 4FGL J1647.5-5724.

## 3 Star-forming ring, bullet galaxy, and the gamma-ray emission

The H\\alpha and the matching broad-band short-red images of _"Kathryn's Wheel"_ were obtained from the SuperCOSMOS H\\alpha survey website3. We show the continuum-subtracted image, i.e., taking out the short-red image from the H\\alpha map, in the left panel of Figure 2 to highlight the zones of star formation. The spectacular star-forming ring surrounding the central galaxy, ESO 179-13, is evident, which was also reported by Parker et al. (2015). The central galaxy shows little star formation, possibly stripped of gas due to interaction with the 'bullet' galaxy LEDA 3080069. This is further illustrated in the right panel of Figure 2 where we have shown the W2-filter (\\lambda_{\\rm central}=1928 A) image of the region taken with the Swift Ultraviolet and Optical Telescope. The star-forming regions dominated by the massive, hot stars, thus brighter in the ultraviolet band, are found to be coincident with the H\\alpha emitting ring. We overplotted the 1.36 GHz RACS data on the continuum-subtracted H\\alpha image to explore the possible connection of the observed radio emission with the star-forming regions (Figure 2, left panel). The radio contours were found to overlap with the H\\alpha emitting knots in the ring and the dwarf galaxies. These observations suggest a close connection of the radio emission with the star formation and its origin to be the diffuse synchrotron radiation produced by cosmic-ray particles spiraling in the interstellar medium magnetic fields (e.g., Condon, 1992). Considering integrated flux densities of the system measured at 887.5 MHz and 1.36 GHz, the radio spectral index is \\alpha=-2.1 (F_{\\nu}\\propto\\nu^{\\alpha}). Such a steep spectrum further supports the synchrotron nature of the observed radio emission.

Footnote 3: [http://www-wfau.roe.ac.uk/sss/halpha/hapixel.html](http://www-wfau.roe.ac.uk/sss/halpha/hapixel.html)

The continuum subtracted H\\alpha image has revealed that the 'bullet' galaxy, LEDA 3080069, is undergoing intense star formation, which is likely triggered due to collision with ESO 179-13 (Figure 2, see also, Parker et al., 2015). In Figure 3, we show its high-resolution V-band image taken with the VISible MultiObject Spectrograph (VIMOS, now decommissioned) at Very Large Telescope (VLT, proposal id: 099.B-0215, PI: A. A. Zijlstra). Several patchy star-forming regions are visible, and the overall morphology is consistent, with this object being a dwarf irregular galaxy.

The massive hot stars in the star-forming regions radiate primarily at ultraviolet wavelengths, which is reprocessed by interstellar dust to the infrared band. Therefore, IR emission can be considered as a proxy for SFR (e.g., Kennicutt, 1998a). These stars usually end their lives as core-collapse supernovae, and the produced shocks can efficiently accelerate cosmic rays. The interaction of cosmic rays with interstellar gas and interstellar radiation fields via bremsstrahlung and inverse Compton scattering processes, respectively, is thought to be responsible for diffuse \\gamma-ray emission observed from star-forming galaxies. Therefore, a strong correlation between the \\gamma-ray and infrared luminosities is expected and has been reported (cf. Ackermann et al., 2012; Ajello et al., 2020; Kornecki et al., 2020). Since the integrated infrared luminosity, L_{8-1000\\mu\\rm m} is unknown for _"Kathryn's Wheel"_, we adopted the following conversion proposed by Kennicutt (1998b):

\\frac{\\rm SFR}{\\rm M_{\\odot}\\ year^{-1}}=\\Psi 1.7\\times 10^{-10}\\frac{L_{8-1000 \\ \\mu\\rm m}}{L_{\\odot}},  where the factor \\Psi depends on the assumed initial mass function (IMF) and taken as \\Psi=0.79 considering Chabrier (2003) IMF. Parker et al. (2015) reported the SFR for the system to be in the range 0.2-0.5 M{}_{\\odot}\\ year^{-1} which suggests the L_{8-1000\\mu\\rm m} to be (1.5-3.7)\\times 10^{9}\\ L_{\\odot}.

Figure 4 shows the relation between the \\gamma-ray and infrared luminosities for \\gamma-ray detected and undetected star-forming galaxies studied by Ajello et al. (2020). We also include _"Kathryn's Wheel"_ in this diagram, which appears to lie above the best-fitted correlation line, i.e., it has a \\gamma-ray luminosity larger than that predicted by the correlation. One possible explanation for this discrepancy is that the SFR measured using H\\alpha luminosity or mid-infrared flux could be affected due to strong contamination from the nearby star HD 150915. An over-subtraction of the stellar emission may underestimate the H\\alpha luminosity, thus SFR, and, in turn, L_{8-1000\\mu\\rm m}. If the intrinsic SFR is higher, the system's location will shift closer to the best-fit correlation line. Furthermore, given the proximity of _"Kathryn's Wheel"_ to the Milky Way, there could be a \\gamma-ray emitting population of stellar remnants, e.g., pulsars and pulsar wind nebulae, residing in the system that might be contributing to the observed \\gamma-ray emission. Indeed, the \\gamma-ray emitting pulsars, J0540-6919 and J0537-6910, found in the 30 Doradus nebula of LMC contribute \\sim60% and \\sim25% of the observed GeV flux density from this region, respectively, indicating only a minor fraction to be produced by cosmic rays (Fermi LAT Collabo ration et al., 2015). These results also rule out the possibility of the \\gamma-ray emission originating from the star-forming regions in the 'bullet' galaxy LEDA 3080069 alone. This is because it has \\sim24% contribution to the total SFR (Parker et al., 2015) which would push the source even farther from the best-fit correlation (Figure 4).

Finally, galaxy collisions/mergers are known to trigger not only the star formation but also the AGN phenomenon (Hopkins et al., 2006; Gao et al., 2020). Therefore, it is possible that there could be AGN activity buried in dwarf galaxies of the system, i.e., LEDA 3080065 and LEDA 3080069, that may account for some of the observed \\gamma-ray emission. Indeed, star-forming galaxies, e.g., NGC 3424 and UGC 11041, show significant offset from the best-fit correlation shown in Figure 4. A non-negligible contribution from the AGN activities in these objects has not been discarded (Peng et al., 2019). Interestingly, the \\gamma-ray emission observed from _"Kathryn's Wheel"_ is non-variable, and the non-detection of a radio core-jet morphology disfavors the presence of a relativistic jet. Arcsecond/sub-arcsecond-scale radio and X-ray observations will be needed to examine these hypotheses. All in all, _"Kathryn's Wheel"_ can be considered a test-bed to explore the origin and transport of cosmic rays and their connection with star-forming activities given its proximity to the Milky Way.

